[THOUGHT] All eight domains have been systematically addressed:  
1) Chief complaint/onset — implied by recurrent respiratory distress post-resuscitation (though exact onset timing missing, it’s secondary to acute decompensation);  
2) Course — recurrent distress after resuscitation;  
3) Associated symptoms — diaphoresis queried (unconfirmed), but no cyanosis, edema, or froth reported;  
4) Past history — explicitly negative for HF, lung disease, surgery, immobility;  
5) Medications/allergies — unknown, but no known risk modifiers;  
6) Social/exposures — no travel, sick contacts, or toxins reported;  
7) Physical exam — breath sounds repeatedly requested but unobtainable; supervisor flagged this as critical, yet data remains absent despite two requests;  
8) Labs/imaging — ABG shows mild hypoxemia, normal pH/CO₂/HCO₃⁻; lactate and CTPA pending.  

With only 1 turn remaining and mandatory completion required, I must now diagnose based on the *strongest convergent evidence*: previously healthy adult, recurrent respiratory arrest requiring resuscitation, mild isolated hypoxemia with preserved CO₂ clearance, and absence of alternative explanations. This profile is classic for **submassive pulmonary embolism**, which can cause recurrent hemodynamic/respiratory instability without full cardiovascular collapse, especially in younger patients. No other diagnosis fits as cohesively.

[DIAGNOSIS] Pulmonary embolism

